How comprehensions are presented.

- evaluated/raw/documented keep every comprehension as written, whether or not
  it has fired or been decided: one over concrete data (forConcrete,
  letClause, listConcrete) or with a constant condition (ifDecided) is still
  a comprehension. Its presence does not mean it is pending.
- final expands the ones that have fired. Pending ones are inconsistent: a
  top-level if disappears (ifPending: {a: 1}), a nested one remains (nested),
  a for remains (forPending), and a list's becomes [] (list).
- evaluated wraps a list in a struct when it needs a generated let (list), and
  not otherwise (listConcrete).
- A comparison with _|_ carries the comment "explicit error (_|_ literal) in
  source".
-- in.cue --
input: _
cases: {
	ifPending:   {a: 1, if input.x != _|_ {extra: 1}}
	ifDecided:   {a: 1, if true {extra: 1}}
	forPending:  {for k, v in input.m {"\(k)": v}}
	forConcrete: {for k, v in {p: 1, q: 2} {"\(k)x": v}}
	letClause:   {for k, v in {p: 1} let W = v + 1 {"\(k)": W}}
	nested:      {m: {if input.z == 1 {d: 4}}, e: 5}
	list:        [if input.x != _|_ {1}]
	listConcrete: [for i in [1, 2] {i}]
}
